  faqs: [
    { question: "I'm in Haridwar and want an IB tutor. What's the first step?", answer: "Tell us the programme, subject, level and exam session your child is sitting, and we come back with tutors who already teach that specific combination. Location drops out of the equation entirely for this search, since nowhere in Haridwar teaches the IB anyway; syllabus experience is all that counts. A trial class always happens before you pay anything, and we swap the tutor if it doesn't click." },
    { question: "Is it possible to find a Cambridge IGCSE tutor who really knows the syllabus?", answer: "Yes, and that's the standard we hold to. The Doon School in Dehradun runs Cambridge, and we match your child by the exact code and tier they study, common ones being Mathematics 0580 Extended or Chemistry 0620, drawing on Cambridge's genuine past papers and mark schemes rather than generic revision material." },
    { question: "Does anyone from IB Gram actually come to our house in Haridwar?", answer: "No, and it's worth being upfront about that. In-person tutoring exists only in Gurugram and a small number of Delhi NCR areas. A Haridwar lesson is a live video call, one student and one tutor, with a shared whiteboard doing the job a physical desk would. It's real tuition delivered to your home over the internet, not a promise of a knock on the door." },
    { question: "What's a realistic budget for an IB or IGCSE tutor if we're in Haridwar?", answer: "That varies by programme, subject, level, session length and how recently your tutor has taught the syllabus in question, and we settle on a number specific to your match before the trial happens. Diploma HL subjects sit at the higher end compared with IGCSE Core work. Since everything runs online, there's no travel cost hidden in the price, and no contract ties you down afterward." },
    { question: "So there genuinely isn't a school in Haridwar teaching IB or IGCSE?", answer: "Correct. Delhi Public School Ranipur and BHEL's Kendriya Vidyalaya both teach CBSE, and Swami Hariharanand Public School at Kankhal is the one CISCE school in the city. Your realistic options are The Doon School in Dehradun, around ninety minutes away, or Mussoorie International School and Woodstock School further up into the hills." },
    { question: "Our child boards at a school near Dehradun. Can lessons work around term breaks?", answer: "Yes, and we hear this often from Haridwar households, given that neither the Diploma nor the Middle Years Programme is taught locally. A tutor gets matched to the exact subject, level and syllabus the boarding school teaches, and sessions get scheduled for holiday windows or, if the school permits it, fixed evening slots during term." },
    { question: "Is there any upfront commitment before we even try a lesson?", answer: "None. Your first step is always a free trial where your child works on a real topic from their own course, at no charge and with zero obligation to book anything further. Only after that does the tutor put together a short plan for month one, which you're free to accept, tweak, or walk away from entirely." },
    { question: "Could a tutor simply write my child's Internal Assessment?", answer: "They won't, though their guidance goes fairly deep short of that line: narrowing down a workable research question, explaining what an assessment criterion is genuinely looking for, helping plan how data gets collected, and giving honest feedback on drafts as they come in. Writing the assessed work itself, or rewriting it, breaks IB's integrity rules outright, so it's simply off the table." },
    { question: "Which Diploma subjects does your Haridwar roster actually cover?", answer: "Pretty much every major subject group a Diploma student boarding away from Haridwar would need: Maths AA and AI at HL and SL, Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Economics, Business Management, English A, Computer Science, plus the TOK and Extended Essay core components." },
    { question: "What about MYP and PYP students, not just Diploma ones?", answer: "Covered too, right across the continuum. At MYP level the focus tends to sit on criterion-based work in sciences and language subjects, along with the Personal Project's ongoing journal, while PYP sessions build reading fluency, number confidence and the inquiry skills the Exhibition eventually calls for." },
    { question: "Can an online tutor genuinely do the job for these boards?", answer: "Given that Haridwar has no physical classroom option to compare it against, the practical answer is yes. Screen sharing, live-marked past papers and recorded explanations a student can replay cover most of what a tutor sitting beside your child would otherwise offer, and the trade-off works firmly in your favour: instead of one nearby generalist, you're choosing from online specialists spread across the whole country." },
    { question: "How do you actually vet the tutors before introducing them?", answer: "Each one is checked on formal qualifications, how recently they've taught that specific subject and level, and how they approach guiding Internal Assessments, all before their name reaches you. Since Haridwar has nothing local to draw on, the search deliberately favours someone with current experience over a generalist willing to give it a go. The trial class is still where you make the final call." },
    { question: "Is there a best time to start tutoring for a Haridwar-based student?", answer: "Starting alongside the course itself works best, Grade 9 for IGCSE or Class 11 for the Diploma, giving enough runway to sort out weak foundations before internal exams and predicted grades arrive. Joining later, even into the final year, still helps, though the plan then narrows sharply to the topics carrying the most exam weight." },
    { question: "We're weighing a CBSE-to-IGCSE switch. Does that make sense from Haridwar?", answer: "It's a genuinely common conversation here, usually tied to a planned move towards The Doon School or a decision to sit as a private candidate. The adjustment is mostly about exam vocabulary rather than subject content, since command words like 'evaluate' and 'justify' need direct teaching well before the switch actually happens." },
    { question: "How do you plan sessions around things like the Kanwar Yatra?", answer: "Most families book weekday evenings and weekend mornings as a baseline, and we build around Haridwar's own calendar on top of that: lighter scheduling through Sawan month when the Kanwar crowds peak, and a further adjustment in any year the Kumbh Mela falls. Slotting in an extra class before an exam series is a quick ask, not a renegotiation." },
    { question: "What happens if the assigned tutor and my child don't get along?", answer: "Just say so, and we'll bring in someone else right away. We don't wait for a scheduled review to act on this; a mismatch gets fixed as soon as you raise it rather than being carried through the term. Since there's no long-term contract either, stepping back from sessions altogether costs you nothing." },
    { question: "Is there any formal link between IB Gram and schools around Haridwar or the IB itself?", answer: "None whatsoever. We run independently, with no endorsement, partnership or representation involving Delhi Public School Ranipur, The Doon School, Mussoorie International School, Woodstock School, the IB Organization or Cambridge Assessment International Education. Every school mentioned here is named solely to explain where local families actually study or board." },
    { question: "Does being close to BHEL, SIDCUL or IIT Roorkee affect how you set things up?", answer: "It shapes what we account for, not how the lesson itself works. We factor in that reaching an IB or Cambridge classroom means a trip out to Dehradun or beyond, and that plenty of families in this belt are juggling JEE or NEET preparation alongside board work given IIT Roorkee's proximity. The actual class still runs online exactly as it would for anyone else." },
  ],

